================================================================================
PROJECT GUARDIAN — SNAPSHOT.doc (AUTHORITATIVE HANDOFF)
================================================================================
This file follows plan §8.1 (required report structure) and §8.2 (quality bar).
Location: ChildSafety_V1.0/ProjectGuardian/SNAPSHOT.doc
Do not split handoff docs: README + this file are the single source of truth here.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0) SNAPSHOT PROTOCOL — INSTRUCTIONS FOR EVERY EDITOR (MANDATORY)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Purpose
  SNAPSHOT.doc is the handoff contract for humans and AI. It must stay accurate
  enough that the next person never has to grep the whole repo to learn state.

Authoritative copy (do not fork content)
  - This file: ProjectGuardian/SNAPSHOT.doc  = FULL report (§0–§10 + quality bar).
  - README.md in this folder = stable runbook + pointer to this file.
  - Parent file: ../SNAPSHOT.doc = short router only; update its “Last updated”
    line when you materially change this file.

When you MUST update this SNAPSHOT.doc (strict cadence)
  - After **each and every** change to the repo: any file added, edited, renamed,
    or deleted — no exceptions. Treat SNAPSHOT as part of the same commit as the code.
  - Before ending a chat session or switching AI models (attach this file).
  - After changing: ports, env var NAMES, schema, API routes, appsettings keys,
    runbook text, or security assumptions.

Automation / commands policy (for AI assistants and tooling)
  - **Do not run shell/terminal commands** except what is strictly required to
    create or edit project files (e.g. writing files is done via editor tools — not
    `npm install`, `pip install`, `dotnet restore`, builds, or tests).
  - **Never install** packages, SDKs, or runtimes automatically. **Ask the human**
    to install prerequisites; list exact names and versions in README.md and
    **MANUAL_STEPS.md**.
  - Do not assume `dotnet build`, `pip install`, or similar have been executed;
    document “user must run locally” in §2 / §7 and in **MANUAL_STEPS.md**.
  - Whenever you tell the user to run a command or do a manual step, **add it to
    MANUAL_STEPS.md** (same session) so guidance stays in the repo.

What to edit each time (checklist)
  1. §1 Metadata — set Date/time (local); Session/task ID; commit hash if git.
  2. §2 Executive summary — what runs E2E; what is stubbed; blockers; build note.
  3. §3 Components — one paragraph per Agent / Brain / Supabase / Dashboard.
  4. §4 File tree — MUST match disk (add/remove paths). Exclude bin/obj/__pycache__.
  5. §5 What was done — numbered, file-anchored bullets (no “fixed stuff”).
  6. §6 Secrets — NAMES only; say “placeholder” where values not set.
  7. §7 How to run — copy/paste commands still valid for Windows paths.
  8. §8 Risks — OCR, HF, DPI, keys, etc.
  9. §9 Next TODO — numbered, actionable in one session (no “keep improving”).
 10. §10 Handoff — best file to open first + 2–4 gotchas.

README.md + MANUAL_STEPS.md sync (plan §8.3)
  - After SNAPSHOT changes, skim README: folder map and env tables must still match.
  - README must keep the line pointing to SNAPSHOT.doc for “current session state.”
  - Any **new** install command, shell command, or manual UI procedure the user must
    run must be written or updated in **MANUAL_STEPS.md** (not only in chat).

Quality gate before you save
  - Complete every item in the §8.2 checklist at the bottom of this file; fix gaps.

Forbidden
  - Pasting API keys, tokens, connection strings, or private URLs with credentials.
  - Vague bullets (“updated code”, “misc fixes”).

Token / context note (for AI sessions)
  - New chats should attach ONLY this file + README.md to restore context cheaply;
    do not rely on chat history alone.

